    ("June 9", "Columba, Aidan and Bede", P, "Three saints of the British Isles: Columba (d. 597), who founded Iona; Aidan (d. 651), who evangelised Northumbria; and Bede (d. 735), the historian of the English Church.", "Scotland, England, missionaries"),
    ("June 10", "Ephrem of Nisibis", A, "Syrian deacon and poet (c. 306-373) who wrote hymns and commentaries and cared for the poor in time of famine. He is a Doctor of the Church in the West.", "Deacons, hymn writers"),
    ("June 11", "Barnabas, Apostle", PA, "A Levite from Cyprus called 'son of encouragement', who introduced Paul to the apostles and travelled with him on his first missionary journey.", "Encouragers, missionaries"),
    ("June 12", "Enmegahbowh", A, "Ojibwe priest and missionary (c. 1810-1902), the first Native American ordained in the Episcopal Church, who protected settlers during the Dakota War of 1862.", "Native Americans, peacemakers"),
    ("June 13", "The First Book of Common Prayer", A, "Published in 1549 under Archbishop Cranmer, the Book of Common Prayer gave the English-speaking church worship in its own language.", "Worshippers"),
    ("June 14", "Basil, Gregory and Macrina", P, "The Cappadocian Fathers Basil the Great and Gregory of Nazianzus and Gregory of Nyssa, and their sister Macrina, taught the Trinity and a life of prayer and service.", "Theologians, teachers"),
    ("June 15", "Evelyn Underhill", A, "English Anglican writer (1875-1941), author of 'Mysticism', and one of the most influential teachers of prayer and retreat in the twentieth century.", "Writers, retreat leaders"),
    ("June 16", "Joseph Butler", A, "Anglican bishop and philosopher (1692-1752) whose 'Analogy of Religion' answered the deists of his time.", "Philosophers, bishops"),
